---
import DocShell from '../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../lib/nav';

const toc = [
   { id: 'run', label: '1. Run axe' },
   { id: 'coverage', label: '2. Check coverage' },
   { id: 'session', label: '3. Start a session' },
   { id: 'read', label: '4. Read the transcript' },
   { id: 'stop', label: '5. Stop the session' },
   { id: 'report', label: 'What you can claim' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Quickstart"
   summary="Take one local route from an axe run to a screen reader transcript. This runs on the virtual target, so it needs nothing beyond the installed package and a running development server."
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section class="split panel" id="run">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">Step 1</p>
            <h2>Run axe on the page</h2>
         </div>
         <p>Start your development server, then run axe against the route you are working on.</p>
         <pre><code>a1 axe --url http://localhost:3000/checkout --level AA --json</code></pre>
         <p>
            The result has four arrays: <code>violations</code>, <code>passes</code>,
            <code>incomplete</code>, and <code>inapplicable</code>. Each violation names the axe
            rule, its impact, the failing selector, and the failing HTML.
         </p>
         <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>"violations": [
  &#123;
    "id": "button-name",
    "impact": "critical",
    "help": "Buttons must have discernible text",
    "nodes": [
      &#123;
        "target": ["button[type=\"button\"]"],
        "html": "&lt;button type=\"button\"&gt;&lt;img src=\"cart.png\" alt=\"\"&gt;&lt;/button&gt;"
      &#125;
    ]
  &#125;
]</code></pre>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">Empty results</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            If a single-page app has not mounted yet, the result carries a <code>warnings</code>
            entry saying the page had too little content to test. Load the route in a browser first
            to confirm it renders.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="coverage">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Step 2</p>
         <h2>Check what the run left out</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         A clean axe run covers the criteria that have axe rules. Ask which criteria those are before
         you draw a conclusion.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 wcag coverage 2.4.3 --json
a1 wcag coverage 1.4.3 --json</code></pre>
      <p>
         Focus Order returns <code>"coverageState": "hybrid"</code> with an empty
         <code>axeRuleIds</code> list. Contrast (Minimum) returns
         <code>"coverageState": "automated"</code> with <code>color-contrast</code>. The first needs
         a screen reader session. The second is already covered by step 1.
      </p>
      <p>
         <a href="/coverage">Coverage</a> explains the four states and where the mapping data comes
         from.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="split panel" id="session">
      <div class="stack">
         <div class="panel-head">
            <p class="panel-kicker">Step 3</p>
            <h2>Start a screen reader session</h2>
         </div>
         <p>
            The virtual target loads the URL into a simulated reader. Pass
            <code>--allow-virtual</code> to say the simulation is what you want.
         </p>
         <pre><code>a1 sr start --target virtual --allow-virtual --url http://localhost:3000/checkout --json</code></pre>
         <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>"session": &#123;
  "sessionId": "drv_c33f1d8f-4f8c-4a78-afe6-f54d7f0b0b50",
  "target": "virtual",
  "targetType": "simulated"
&#125;</code></pre>
         <p>Copy the <code>sessionId</code>. Every later command takes it as <code>--session</code>.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="split-aside">
         <div class="tag">Skip the flag</div>
         <p class="aside-note">
            Set <code>A11IED_DRIVE_SESSION</code> to the session id and the other
            <code>sr</code> commands pick it up without <code>--session</code>.
         </p>
      </div>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="read">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Step 4</p>
         <h2>Step through the page and read the log</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         <code>sr next</code> moves to the next element and returns what the reader announced.
         <code>sr read</code> returns the current state without moving.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr next --session drv_c33f1d8f --json
a1 sr next --session drv_c33f1d8f --json
a1 sr logs --session drv_c33f1d8f --json</code></pre>
      <pre data-label="OUTPUT"><code>"spokenPhraseLog": [
  "document",
  "heading, Checkout, level 1",
  "form",
  "Email",
  "textbox, Email, required",
  "Card number",
  "textbox, Card number",
  "button, Pay now",
  "button",
  "end of form"
]</code></pre>
      <p>
         Read the log as a sequence. The email field announces its required state. The last button
         announces as <code>button</code> with nothing after it, which matches the
         <code>button-name</code> violation from step 1.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="stop">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Step 5</p>
         <h2>Stop the session</h2>
      </div>
      <pre><code>a1 sr stop --session drv_c33f1d8f --json</code></pre>
      <p>
         Stopping shuts down the driver process, finishes any recording, and deletes the session
         state file. Sessions outlive the shell that started them, so stop each one you start.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note" id="report">
      <div class="panel-head">
         <p class="panel-kicker">Result</p>
         <h2>What this run supports</h2>
      </div>
      <p>
         You have an axe result for the 72 rules mapped to level AA, and a transcript of the
         announcement order for one route on one target.
      </p>
      <p>
         It is not a conformance claim. The virtual reader is a simulation, and 12 of the 24 level
         AA criteria have no automated coverage at all. Move to
         <a href="/guides/screen-reader">VoiceOver or NVDA</a> when the real behavior is what you
         need to check.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
